l over one's  environment  control over one's uh again disease  course to some extent and how they how  they manage it  and then of course there's the factors  that just relate to the disease itself  so we know that our patients with  chronic disease report some of the  lowest quality of life measures  that actually result from the disease  states and one thing to keep in mind for  those of you that are using animal  models of disease these very highly  representative animal models of disease  have  significant welfare implications that we  need to keep  at the forefront of our minds so  one of the things that i like to keep in  mind is that what when we think about  our model design the more  intentional we are about it it's our  first and best defense it's study rigor  and animal welfare so again improving  our likelihood of benefit  improving likelihood of successful  translation and there are many things  that go into the state of our animals  that i wanted to summarize here we  talked about how sickness and disease so  the actual model itself certainly  factors in  a big one for those of us that are again  at that safety and efficacy  side of of use in in primates the  medical surgical interventions  themselves and post-substantial verdin  but also just being a research subject  of course so the handling in the  environment that we impose on our  research subjects  and this and the way that it affects  their sociality all of those factor into  their behavioral psychological and  physiological health  and one of the things that i think is  really key to keep in mind also in using  primates is their heterogeneity  is is really a strength of the model  right so  when we think about translational  strengths we don't want to we don't want  to drive that piece of it out  and certainly the ability to perform  interventions the way that we anticipate  with our clinical patients  is also a strength and again bringing  back in that  bringing that back to the forefront  r