mental  design so it increases the support of  care that we can do for our animals  and it really enhances well being by  bringing them closer to the clinical  situation so we can do things like  rehydration  tpn and again as i said monitor with a  frequency  that that really allows us to intervene  early  and in a very effective way from the  welfare perspective  indirectly what we see is is the support  and coping with the research  environment so you you can't  evidently predict everything that's  going to happen on study  certainly and what what this allows us  to do is have a  a general positive cognitive bias that  we see from from animals towards  caregivers  so they associate these interactions  with caregivers as rewarding  and what we can assume from that is it  reduces the perception of pain of course  but also allows us to if we don't have  something that we anticipated  be able to do that with an animal who  was ready to cooperate  one of the next things we decided to  really look at having seen the cytokine  data and this is  work again by one of my students  got hunter opler that's uh having a look  at the impact of cooperation versus  chemical restraint during intensive  sampling  so what we would see in this situation  again of a typical  talk study for example and  what you see here is the timeline so  pre-sampling event for baseline health  physiologic assessment  day zero sampling and test article  administration  and then relatively frequent sampling  followed by less frequent regular  sampling for the duration of the study  and we had six animals in the in the  group that was chemically restrained  three males three females  and six animals in the cooperative group  so a total of 12  young adult monkeys and this is a 42-day  study  and what you can see aside from from  what i've already shown retrospectively  with the with the changes in immunology  is as we  as we look at sort of again standard  outcome measures that we would report in  a regu