ement program and it's quite  striking how you see the difference  in weight loss or or essentially  having animals really thrive under this  regimen and again the difference being  behavioral management  and we really aim to show that this is  not related to our metabolic  intervention because  as you see in in panel c the  the insulin exposure of the animals is  very similar so it's not a deficit in  insulin  it's not a deficit in in glucose  disposal we see  again that there's relative little  difference between glucose disposal it's  improved of course  in the behavioral management group for  the reasons that we know as i showed  earlier  but again the real difference being the  the number of sedation events or number  of  restraint events these animals were  exposed to making a big difference in  primary outcomes on the study  so experimental designs can certainly be  influenced by many factors  and and we know that researchers safety  and infectious disease studies or  or thinking about the ease in which we  we sample are big factors in in how we  set this up  but certainly some of the the stress on  on research subjects related to these  decisions are important to think about  because we know that it can ultimately  confound study endpoints related to  immune response  and certainly safety two of which we  talked about as very  objective examples today that are very  critical in our ability to evaluate new  drugs or devices  behavioral management can foster  resilience in our animals and certainly  improves welfare  and i think it's important to always  acknowledge that translational failures  can happen despite our best  most rigorous science and in using  reverse translation or thinking about  how we  how we look at our clinical situation  and and what works  what doesn't work and thinking through  those refinements are really key to  enhancing our modeling  and of course deeply characterizing our  models avoids our misinterpretation of  study endpoints so 